1
00:00:00,398 --> 00:00:19,852
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, so, so, like, just to ask about the question about sotāpanna, because people obviously want to practice and find a teacher with a right view, that's probably a criterion for practice, and how they can judge who to follow, who to listen.

2
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:24,896
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to realize that you don't have the criteria for what right is.

3
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:29,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have a criteria for what teacher knows what they talk about and which one of them doesn't.

4
00:00:30,271 --> 00:00:30,631
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't.

5
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:35,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The only criteria a person has is this is agreeable and this is disagreeable.

6
00:00:36,573 --> 00:00:40,174
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not necessarily always corresponding to this is right and this is wrong.

7
00:00:41,555 --> 00:00:43,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes disagreeable things are right.

8
00:00:43,815 --> 00:00:45,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes agreeable things are wrong.

9
00:00:45,616 --> 00:00:49,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes agreeable things are right, depending on what you find agreeable or disagreeable.

10
00:00:50,817 --> 00:00:59,979
[SPEAKER_00]: And you kind of... usually it's going to be this person is acting in a way that fits with my image of what I think.

11
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:01,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you question your image.

12
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:02,480
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know what a sotāpanna is.

13
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:04,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It has... you're going to have some notion.

14
00:01:05,361 --> 00:01:08,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some... some... some ājahn will be more inspiring to you than the other.

15
00:01:09,962 --> 00:01:10,502
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's fine.

16
00:01:10,622 --> 00:01:11,783
[SPEAKER_01]: There has to be a starting point.

17
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:16,524
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to stop there and now just blindly defend that ājahn and not listen to any other one.

18
00:01:17,584 --> 00:01:18,084
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't see.

19
00:01:18,344 --> 00:01:20,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is this more inspiring to me?

20
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:30,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to start, as I always say, with something that is least likely to be wrong, which is the suttas themselves, but that doesn't mean they read plainly, clearly.

21
00:01:30,662 --> 00:01:43,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody is basically responsible for their own interpretation of most of the suttas, because they've been, you know, it's been so long time ago and they're so general and so condensed in meaning that, yeah, same passage can mean one thing and completely opposite thing for somebody else.

22
00:01:44,965 --> 00:01:46,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be honest with yourself.

23
00:01:48,715 --> 00:01:49,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I free from suffering?

24
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:49,995
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

25
00:01:50,055 --> 00:01:51,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I don't have the right criteria.

26
00:01:51,616 --> 00:01:53,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I better be careful when I choose to follow agreeable.

27
00:01:54,356 --> 00:01:57,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And I better be careful when I choose to dismiss the disagreeable.

28
00:01:59,877 --> 00:02:06,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So, having just suttas available, not a teacher, is still some hope for attaining the right view?

29
00:02:06,239 --> 00:02:13,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, but there is a... there is a big gap between the suttas and when people approach it.

30
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:20,313
[SPEAKER_01]: There usually, on practical level, there has to be something in between, at least to direct you towards the suttas, how to read them rightly and regard them rightly.

31
00:02:21,073 --> 00:02:24,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's where we spoke in one of the previous talks, exactly.

32
00:02:25,836 --> 00:02:35,780
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even those who said, you know, they're practicing alone and just suttas, they also have nilavira as a starting point, like... Well, yeah, yeah, there has to be something.

33
00:02:37,321 --> 00:02:38,582
[SPEAKER_02]: And if they don't find any...

34
00:02:39,703 --> 00:02:45,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see there, that's it, that's it.

35
00:02:46,627 --> 00:02:48,348
[SPEAKER_01]: If they don't find, they won't find it.

36
00:02:49,589 --> 00:02:53,111
[SPEAKER_01]: The only time they realize they found it, is when they become āryas themselves.

37
00:02:53,331 --> 00:02:54,392
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, they won't need it.

38
00:02:56,433 --> 00:03:00,095
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't be sure, unless you're a sotāpanna, that you, your teacher is a sotāpanna.

39
00:03:00,875 --> 00:03:02,656
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you're a sotāpanna, then you will know.

40
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:05,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you wouldn't have been a sotāpanna.

41
00:03:07,396 --> 00:03:09,836
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a question of finding the right teacher.

42
00:03:10,036 --> 00:03:13,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

43
00:03:13,157 --> 00:03:15,077
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, until they find the right teacher...

44
00:03:15,097 --> 00:03:17,638
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll never find the right teacher if they're looking for the right teacher.

45
00:03:18,478 --> 00:03:21,458
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to find the right view on account of the teacher.

46
00:03:22,539 --> 00:03:28,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know that you found the right teacher when you stop needing it.

47
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:33,580
[SPEAKER_02]: And what can a putujana teacher can do for?

48
00:03:33,981 --> 00:03:34,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

49
00:03:34,961 --> 00:03:36,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Can inspire people in virtue.

50
00:03:37,857 --> 00:03:42,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's highly unlikely they would be inspiring someone in virtue without inspiring them in the further wrong view.

51
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:48,506
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's possible.

52
00:03:48,806 --> 00:04:06,341
[SPEAKER_01]: If a patujan is honest, transparent, and says, I don't know where you're suffering, but the way I understood the suttas is that sense-restraint is really necessary, and really important, and even if you don't get anywhere, as the Buddha said, if you spend the rest of the days of your life in tears of pains of sense-restraint, it's still better, and you're heading for heavens and so on.

53
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:19,494
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is, once you get to the stage of being a teacher, you are now in contact with praise, honor, gains.

54
00:04:20,694 --> 00:04:22,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's going to corrupt you.

55
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:24,697
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're... unless you kind of...

56
00:04:24,837 --> 00:04:25,537
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's no right view.

57
00:04:25,557 --> 00:04:27,659
[SPEAKER_00]: If there's no right view, that's going to corrupt you.

58
00:04:27,939 --> 00:04:30,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, you already corrupted yourself even before that.

59
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:34,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You were teaching freedom from suffering while knowing you don't know the freedom from suffering.

60
00:04:35,443 --> 00:04:37,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even before praise and blame, you were already corrupted.

61
00:04:41,830 --> 00:04:43,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean, like I said that in previous talk.

62
00:04:44,111 --> 00:04:50,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you kind of inadvertently... Oh, I found this helpful, but you're presenting it as dhamma, but you don't know whether that's dhamma or not.

63
00:04:54,096 --> 00:04:55,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a very unwholesome thing to do.

64
00:04:57,298 --> 00:04:59,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't accidentally be right.

65
00:04:59,699 --> 00:05:04,762
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot accidentally stumble upon nibbāna and then become enlightened.

66
00:05:10,993 --> 00:05:24,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the corruption started by giving in to the, you know, established norms of whatever teacher today is, knowing or not knowing or ignoring the fact that you don't know the way of suffering yourself.

67
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:27,833
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a plainest contradiction in terms that it can be.

68
00:05:28,514 --> 00:05:31,037
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know the escape and here you are preaching the escape.

69
00:05:35,073 --> 00:05:39,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Some teachers probably think they know the way of suffering there are.

70
00:05:39,056 --> 00:05:50,223
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure some would kind of, yeah, think they know, but many times, many times, I heard, like, senior monks say, I'm not a sotāpanna, but then spend an hour giving you dhamma talk, telling you how to practice dhamma.

71
00:05:51,103 --> 00:05:52,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just I'm not.

72
00:05:52,204 --> 00:05:52,744
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nobody.

73
00:05:52,764 --> 00:05:54,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

74
00:05:54,685 --> 00:05:55,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's nothing to attain.

75
00:05:55,686 --> 00:05:56,567
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing.

76
00:05:56,667 --> 00:05:57,967
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you practice, the less you know.

77
00:05:59,308 --> 00:05:59,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And things like that.

78
00:05:59,909 --> 00:06:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And then go on and teach.

79
00:06:01,510 --> 00:06:01,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice.

80
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:14,983
[SPEAKER_01]: That's irrelevant.

81
00:06:15,824 --> 00:06:18,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you declare or not, or whatever, it's irrelevant.

82
00:06:21,087 --> 00:06:22,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, well, I mean, it depends.

83
00:06:22,348 --> 00:06:24,049
[SPEAKER_02]: It is your place to judge that for yourself.

84
00:06:31,098 --> 00:06:35,822
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not your place to judge that for everybody else in the world, but you had to judge for yourself.

85
00:06:37,003 --> 00:06:40,905
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have to judge what you just judged for yourself and not take that for granted either.

86
00:06:42,347 --> 00:06:45,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And then see why you judged it in a certain manner and not in the other one.

87
00:06:47,891 --> 00:06:49,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you will become transparent.

88
00:06:57,562 --> 00:07:05,868
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just thinking there's so few places in the world where one can ordain and practice, and so many choices, and then...

89
00:07:06,589 --> 00:07:20,019
[SPEAKER_02]: So, basically, you go to any monastery where you can get training vinaya, and then you have to just be quite independent in some way, but there's also can have a disadvantage of too independence, you become arrogant.

90
00:07:20,959 --> 00:07:23,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, monastery doesn't make you practice.

91
00:07:23,921 --> 00:07:25,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-monastery doesn't make you non-practice.

92
00:07:27,765 --> 00:07:28,705
[SPEAKER_01]: You yourself do so.

93
00:07:30,325 --> 00:07:45,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're looking for a place so you can practice, means you're looking for practice at the wrong place.

94
00:07:48,529 --> 00:07:50,069
[SPEAKER_01]: No, well, that's why we said that before.

95
00:07:50,089 --> 00:07:55,450
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to recognize it... you're fully responsible for practicing or non-practicing.

96
00:07:58,224 --> 00:08:03,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not in the monastery, you're fully responsible for restraint or non-restraint.

97
00:08:04,049 --> 00:08:07,212
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not mean, because I'm not in the monastery, I don't need to be restrained.

98
00:08:08,113 --> 00:08:11,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Or because, if I get into monastery, I will automatically be restrained.

99
00:08:14,739 --> 00:08:20,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start thinking like that, then you really... certainly, in the beginning, won't matter as much where you are.

100
00:08:21,231 --> 00:08:25,552
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense of, like, I really need this environment, and there is no practice without it.

101
00:08:26,032 --> 00:08:34,014
[SPEAKER_01]: It will only matter in that indirect sense whereby, are you constantly bombarded with things you have to say no to, and restrain, and endure, or are you not?

102
00:08:35,135 --> 00:08:48,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can actually reach, find certain, quite a lot of circum... like, like, uh... ways of living, even without being in the monastery, they would be quite secluded and non-distracting, and not really, um...

103
00:08:49,402 --> 00:08:50,943
[SPEAKER_01]: I can even see the practice lack of people.

104
00:08:50,963 --> 00:08:51,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Wow.

105
00:09:05,995 --> 00:09:09,197
[SPEAKER_01]: And they change the place, the practice is gone.

106
00:09:09,617 --> 00:09:13,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even worse, they leave the monastery, still as a monk, and they disrobe.

107
00:09:14,959 --> 00:09:20,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even the rules have not been kept internally, it was all done by the circumstances and just following what others do and say.

108
00:09:21,401 --> 00:09:23,302
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment you step out of that bubble, you're done.

109
00:09:24,162 --> 00:09:26,884
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no other criteria, reference point, making independence.

110
00:09:26,924 --> 00:09:29,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Surrender to the form, as I was told once.

111
00:09:29,925 --> 00:09:30,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Surrender to the form.

112
00:09:30,845 --> 00:09:31,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Surrender to the form.

113
00:09:31,946 --> 00:09:32,966
[SPEAKER_00]: The, you know, the...

114
00:09:33,669 --> 00:09:36,510
[SPEAKER_00]: The kind of structures of the monastery surrender to all that.

115
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:37,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Just do it.

116
00:09:37,150 --> 00:09:37,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop thinking about it.

117
00:09:38,011 --> 00:09:38,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... exactly.

118
00:09:39,771 --> 00:09:40,592
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a good advice.

119
00:09:40,612 --> 00:09:41,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't take responsibility.

120
00:09:42,012 --> 00:09:45,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Let the monastery be your... yeah, your responsibility.

121
00:09:47,334 --> 00:09:52,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is not quite what the Buddha meant when he said, be an island for yourself.

122
00:09:52,416 --> 00:09:53,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

123
00:09:57,139 --> 00:10:03,082
[SPEAKER_02]: That even critical monks wanting seclusion, they just should surrender to pujas and gathering.

124
00:10:03,262 --> 00:10:03,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

125
00:10:03,863 --> 00:10:06,924
[SPEAKER_01]: People have their reasons.

126
00:10:07,044 --> 00:10:23,793
[SPEAKER_03]: Because once you find that right place, the right place of practice,

127
00:10:25,627 --> 00:10:34,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, it's... it reveals how ridiculous or how unnecessarily external circumstances are...

128
00:10:34,936 --> 00:10:36,117
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean the genuine right place.

129
00:10:36,137 --> 00:10:37,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, when you find the real right place of practice.

130
00:10:37,378 --> 00:10:39,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, absolutely, absolutely, yeah.

131
00:10:39,541 --> 00:10:40,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Internal place.

132
00:10:40,582 --> 00:10:42,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And in short, it's talking about the real home.

133
00:10:42,824 --> 00:10:43,524
[SPEAKER_00]: The real home.

134
00:10:43,565 --> 00:10:44,606
[SPEAKER_00]: The real home, yeah.

135
00:10:45,546 --> 00:10:47,068
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you were saying before.

136
00:10:47,188 --> 00:10:47,869
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the...

137
00:10:51,303 --> 00:10:56,186
[SPEAKER_00]: What's important, what's wholesome and unwholesome, has nothing to do with the things out there?

138
00:10:57,447 --> 00:11:02,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the things that it had something to do with, are the things you abandon by keeping the precepts.

139
00:11:02,991 --> 00:11:05,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's on to you to develop the principle within that.

140
00:11:09,195 --> 00:11:12,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, literally, like, again, just think about it.

141
00:11:13,178 --> 00:11:15,740
[SPEAKER_01]: If your place, if your practice depends on a place,

142
00:11:17,869 --> 00:11:18,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have a say over places?

143
00:11:18,909 --> 00:11:20,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have a say over duration?

144
00:11:20,049 --> 00:11:22,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have a say over the environment not changing?

145
00:11:22,630 --> 00:11:24,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you do, will you have that say forever?

146
00:11:25,271 --> 00:11:25,431
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

147
00:11:25,791 --> 00:11:27,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, sooner or later, that has to go.

148
00:11:28,251 --> 00:11:30,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, then, your practice will have to go with it.

149
00:11:31,252 --> 00:11:36,773
[SPEAKER_01]: I always say, like, no, imagine, like, today, you'll end up in a hospital.

150
00:11:38,173 --> 00:11:40,274
[SPEAKER_00]: By the end of the day, you have to be hospitalized for something.

151
00:11:41,254 --> 00:11:45,775
[SPEAKER_01]: How would your mind... would your mind remain equanimous, indifferent, in the same sense it is on top of a mountain?

152
00:11:46,553 --> 00:11:48,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it should be, if you've been practicing rightly.

153
00:11:49,915 --> 00:11:57,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Bombarded by elements, enduring the words and company and discomfort, it still should not make any difference, because that's not where the difference is.

154
00:12:00,824 --> 00:12:06,688
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the thought of it already freaks you out, means, well, there you go, loads of work for you to do, and that's exactly what needs to be dealt with.

155
00:12:08,224 --> 00:12:20,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean, like, even when people go in the forest in seclusion, they do it out of that projected external reason, which is just because it pleases them, makes things easier at the time, so you don't have to deal with anything else.

156
00:12:20,708 --> 00:12:22,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And look, look, all the suttas talk about seclusion.

157
00:12:22,809 --> 00:12:27,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, once you have the right view, sure, you'll know exactly where the place of that seclusion is.

158
00:12:28,190 --> 00:12:30,311
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, it's a relative thing, seclusion.

159
00:12:33,012 --> 00:12:35,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, some people can live on top of a mountain and still live in company.

160
00:12:36,550 --> 00:12:39,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people can live surrounded by people, but be completely secluded.

161
00:12:41,272 --> 00:12:46,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the company, again, is internal basis for desire for distraction, or non-distraction.

162
00:12:48,096 --> 00:12:48,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

163
00:12:48,856 --> 00:12:51,938
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be very curious, not just... Yeah.

164
00:12:52,038 --> 00:12:54,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Physically boarding yourself, you know, in a stone cell.

165
00:12:54,779 --> 00:12:55,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

166
00:12:55,480 --> 00:12:56,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Padded stone cell.

167
00:12:57,341 --> 00:13:00,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Where you won't be able to hear, see or... nothing.

168
00:13:00,702 --> 00:13:01,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing of the external world.

169
00:13:03,448 --> 00:13:09,132
[SPEAKER_03]: You can also say that that place of practice is incomparable to any other, to a physical place.

170
00:13:09,152 --> 00:13:10,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because it's not a place, exactly.

171
00:13:10,973 --> 00:13:12,114
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a physical place.

172
00:13:12,154 --> 00:13:18,798
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it overrides all physical, beautiful places in the world that you could ever imagine, ever.

173
00:13:19,838 --> 00:13:26,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Some, you know, secluded mountain stream, the deers... Well, there was the suttas as well, when the Buddha... What was that?

174
00:13:28,029 --> 00:13:36,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, when the man saw the bed by the road, like a bed of leaves and twigs, he says, well, whoever slept here last night must have had a rough night.

175
00:13:36,957 --> 00:13:40,921
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, no, among those who sleep well, I'm the foremost who sleeps well.

176
00:13:41,662 --> 00:13:48,148
[SPEAKER_01]: And the man didn't understand and he says, imagine you're a king with like, I don't know, 15 wives that attend to your every need and

177
00:13:49,497 --> 00:13:55,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the high luxurious bed and for everything you can possibly imagine in terms of the place, comfort, sense, desire.

178
00:13:56,439 --> 00:14:12,122
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, wouldn't on some occasion be, like that king, wouldn't his mind be gripped by something unpleasant on account of somebody said something or somebody didn't do something, so that fever, he could not sleep at night, no amount of wives could attend to him for the... he would be gripped by that ill will or lust or pain.

179
00:14:12,522 --> 00:14:13,903
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yes, yes, that's possible.

180
00:14:14,483 --> 00:14:19,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, but what about those who are incapable of experiencing that kind of internal pain, grief, fever,

181
00:14:20,685 --> 00:14:22,406
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

182
00:14:22,446 --> 00:14:25,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's actually the root of the disturbance.

183
00:14:26,009 --> 00:14:30,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Not where you sleep, or how you sleep, or what elements you endure, or what elements you don't endure.

184
00:14:32,955 --> 00:14:34,656
[SPEAKER_03]: So, circumstances become irrelevant.

185
00:14:35,577 --> 00:14:36,758
[SPEAKER_01]: All become equal in places.

186
00:14:36,778 --> 00:14:37,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, exactly.

187
00:14:37,599 --> 00:14:38,259
[SPEAKER_01]: You went beyond them.

188
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:42,843
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, the whole... Was it Sābhāsa Sutta?

189
00:14:42,863 --> 00:14:47,947
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole set of kilesas to be abandoned simply by enduring them.

190
00:14:49,525 --> 00:14:52,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Heat, cold, words of others, discomfort.

191
00:14:52,746 --> 00:14:53,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Just have to endure it.

192
00:14:53,946 --> 00:14:54,866
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you become strong.

193
00:14:54,906 --> 00:14:55,766
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you abandon it.

194
00:15:02,907 --> 00:15:15,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, like when the... when a person's mind gets touched by the pleasant feeling, the body gets touched by the pleasant feeling, as in the possibility for pleasure arises, they cannot resist it.

195
00:15:16,050 --> 00:15:18,010
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot help but crave towards it.

196
00:15:19,944 --> 00:15:24,388
[SPEAKER_01]: When they're touched by the unpleasant feeling, again, they could just bear it.

197
00:15:25,569 --> 00:15:26,349
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always an option.

198
00:15:26,890 --> 00:15:27,330
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can't.

199
00:15:27,470 --> 00:15:28,911
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't help crave against it.

200
00:15:29,952 --> 00:15:30,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why they suffer.

201
00:15:30,913 --> 00:15:36,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they're touched in the first place, but because they can't help either crave for more or for less of it.

202
00:15:38,219 --> 00:15:43,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how deeply circumstances are irrelevant in that sense.

203
00:15:43,983 --> 00:15:45,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Even your craving is not in circumstances.

204
00:15:46,525 --> 00:15:46,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

205
00:15:47,085 --> 00:15:52,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that... the agreeable and disagreeable is not... it's... in the circumstance, it's not the route for lust and aversion.

206
00:15:53,509 --> 00:15:55,571
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your own ignorance of agreeable and disagreeable.

207
00:15:57,972 --> 00:16:01,494
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can only become apparent on the basis of sensory strength and the precepts.

208
00:16:08,438 --> 00:16:14,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one criteria that Kutujana can use for finding the right teacher or the teaching

209
00:16:14,989 --> 00:16:17,470
[SPEAKER_01]: is... why do I want to practice dhamma in the first place?

210
00:16:19,390 --> 00:16:25,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Be very, very clear what you want, and then you will become clearer as to what you need.

211
00:16:25,692 --> 00:16:27,713
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know what you need, because you don't know what you really want.

212
00:16:29,233 --> 00:16:34,155
[SPEAKER_02]: I think most of us start... people start practicing for the wrong reason.

213
00:16:35,155 --> 00:16:40,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe we're just inspiring... Well, not being clear about your reasons is a wrong reason in itself.

214
00:16:40,177 --> 00:16:40,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

215
00:16:43,078 --> 00:16:44,578
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do you want to practice dhamma?

216
00:16:46,688 --> 00:16:48,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because I want to be free from suffering.

217
00:16:48,149 --> 00:16:57,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, I'll seek for the teachings that talk about freedom from suffering, and not talk about some random anecdotal stories and something that will entertain me and help me fall asleep.

218
00:17:02,759 --> 00:17:03,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You can question any further.

219
00:17:04,340 --> 00:17:08,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I value freedom from suffering, while I'm not being free from suffering.

220
00:17:08,823 --> 00:17:13,426
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were to think about it, how would that freedom from suffering be developed?

221
00:17:14,401 --> 00:17:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Through suffering or through extreme pleasure?

222
00:17:16,142 --> 00:17:17,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more likely?

223
00:17:18,143 --> 00:17:19,184
[SPEAKER_01]: You already get the answer.

224
00:17:20,545 --> 00:17:25,769
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are valuable to... to be, you know, obtained in the world, do you have to work for them?

225
00:17:26,389 --> 00:17:28,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you just get them through pure pleasure?

226
00:17:28,510 --> 00:17:29,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more often?

227
00:17:29,531 --> 00:17:30,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more common?

228
00:17:31,792 --> 00:17:32,453
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you know.

229
00:17:32,473 --> 00:17:32,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

230
00:17:32,753 --> 00:17:39,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I should then seek for that with... for the teachings that tell me... talk about freedom from suffering and also make me feel unpleasant.

231
00:17:44,087 --> 00:17:53,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can, even with an ignorant, putuginous mind, you can still develop kind of accurate criteria, if you're willing to accept the discomfort of it.

